00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:32.992
And this is--

00:00:32.992 --> 00:00:34.880
VILLE AIKAS: And
I'm Ville Aikas.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:36.865
I'm a software engineer
from Seattle working

00:00:36.865 --> 00:00:39.198
on Deployment Manager and a
couple of other Cloud config

00:00:39.198 --> 00:00:40.030
things.

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:42.196
ANIL DHAWAN: So what we're
going to talk about today

00:00:42.196 --> 00:00:46.630
is this concept of no more
dead software deployments.

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:50.100
I think we all know that
deploying software is already

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:50.950
hard enough.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.650
Anyone disagree?

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:53.682
Oh, got one.

00:00:53.682 --> 00:00:55.450
Couple dissenters.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.490
We should talk.

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.410
Deploying software's
already hard enough,

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:01.180
but it turns out when
you move into cloud,

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.760
deploying your infrastructure
can often be harder.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.450
And so what we're
going to do today

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.560
is talk about some
of the philosophies

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.120
that have emerged over time,
especially when it comes

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.025
to cloud, to make this easier.

00:01:14.025 --> 00:01:16.150
And we're going to show
you some of the tools we've

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:19.760
built on Google Cloud Platform
that you can use to implement

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.860
those philosophies to make sure
that you're no longer deploying

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:24.909
dead software.

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:26.450
And then finally,
we're going to show

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:28.866
you some of the improvements
that we've done to the Google

00:01:28.866 --> 00:01:31.290
Cloud Launcher product
to integrate and tie it

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.840
all together.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.160
So for the first
time in cloud, you

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.380
have this unprecedented
ability to spin up

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:43.310
a whole host of resources,
from virtual machines,

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.630
to firewalls, to
disks, in one zone,

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:48.330
across multiple zones
around the world.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:50.424
And that's fantastic.

00:01:50.424 --> 00:01:52.840
And what happens is when you
get these powerful tools that

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.130
let you spin up these resources
in a matter of minutes,

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.330
is you tend to use it a lot.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:00.190
You no longer are
constrained or confined

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:04.070
to plugging in ethernet cables
and managing on data center.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:06.730
So you're now trying
different things.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:08.900
You might be experimenting
with a staging

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:10.539
cluster in one environment.

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:12.080
You might be ramping
up a production

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:15.442
cluster in a different zone.

00:02:15.442 --> 00:02:16.275
And that's exciting.

00:02:16.275 --> 00:02:18.800
And it leads to
complex architectures,

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.740
but it also gives you some
great benefits, like agility.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:26.820
You can finally take an
idea, put it into practice,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.040
see if it works.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.520
And if it doesn't, tear it down.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.390
Not a lot of cost to it.

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:34.440
So the flexibility
you get is fantastic.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:38.330
But as Spider-Man was
told, with all that power

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:40.600
comes a great sense
of responsibility.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:41.160
So great.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.370
You've spun up all
of these resources.

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:45.130
And you've SSH'd
into those machines.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:47.210
And you've tuned
that kernel parameter

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:50.080
to make sure the IOPS
are going to be great.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:53.100
But now when you want to
move to a larger system,

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:56.920
or scale down, or replicate
that setup, what do you do?

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:01.480
And so the default state is
what we call dead software.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.590
And dead software comes
about because you've

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.230
lost control of what you did.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:07.140
You've created these
unique snowflakes.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.980
And they were great
when they were there,

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.140
but you no longer know
how to get back to it.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:12.640
And what this means
is if you're not

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.540
going to have a way to get
back into that configuration

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.000
repeatably, you're
going to be vulnerable

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.270
because your software is
going to go out of date.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.603
And you're going to get things
like configuration drift.

00:03:23.603 --> 00:03:25.140
Because what you
had in your mind

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:27.880
is not what's running
in production.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:29.522
Well, as you can
imagine, some people

00:03:29.522 --> 00:03:30.980
have tried to solve
these problems.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.160
And there are tools that do so.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:34.780
But they're not a
whole lot better

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.030
in terms of solving a problem.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.279
And I think what they lead
to is potentially something

00:03:38.279 --> 00:03:40.230
worse than dead software.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.340
And that is zombie software.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:44.210
What these tools
try to do is they

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.420
try to patch a
running environment

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.730
to try to keep it up to date.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:50.790
In concept, that's great.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:53.100
But we all know the
reality is when you're

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.050
patching a running
system, you're

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:59.390
never going to be able to
make it work 100% of the time.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:03.350
Well, when it fails, it's
really difficult to roll back.

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:05.849
It's equivalent to trying
to change the tires on a car

00:04:05.849 --> 00:04:06.640
while it's running.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.811
It's something you probably
wouldn't want to do.

00:04:09.811 --> 00:04:10.310
So great.

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:11.140
So we've seen this.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.260
We've seen this kind of model
happen in the on-prem world.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.370
And when you move into
cloud, this problem

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:17.510
becomes exacerbated.

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:19.850
So there's got to be a
better solution, right?

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:21.230
Well, it turns out there is.

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:22.730
And it's actually
quite fascinating.

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:24.690
In the last few
years, this pattern

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.050
has emerged around
immutable infrastructure.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:29.220
And if you saw the
Spinnaker talk today,

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.560
you know exactly what
I'm talking about.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.680
And the goal of
immutable infrastructure

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.000
is to say, hey, let's no
longer change the tires

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:38.350
while the car's running.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.760
Let's instead take
all the components

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.760
that comprise your
application, bundle them up

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:46.436
into one immutable image.

00:04:46.436 --> 00:04:49.560
And you can do this through
the notion of a bakery.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.530
And this whole process is
co-developed in the industry.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:55.395
Externally, we've seen
folks like Spinnaker,

00:04:55.395 --> 00:04:58.542
by Netflix, but also
internally at Google,

00:04:58.542 --> 00:05:00.250
we've been doing these
patterns ourselves

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:05.070
for several years with Borg,
Borg config, and Midas.

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:06.040
So this is great.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:08.510
Once you have an immutable
software package,

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:11.210
you have an atomic unit
that you know is good,

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.310
and you can trust it.

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:14.950
But that's only
part of the battle.

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:17.120
You've got this atomic
unit, but now you

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.880
want to make sure that you
can repeatably deploy it

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:22.730
in a multitude of environments.

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:24.989
There aren't many tools that
can do that really well.

00:05:24.989 --> 00:05:27.530
And that's what we're going to
talk to you about today, which

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:29.529
is how do you implement
immutable infrastructure

00:05:29.529 --> 00:05:31.870
on Google Cloud Platform?

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:34.090
We're going to show you
our tool of implementing

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:35.449
this philosophy.

00:05:35.449 --> 00:05:36.740
It's called Deployment Manager.

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:38.781
And we're going to walk
through a set of examples

00:05:38.781 --> 00:05:41.560
of how to get a quick VM up and
running, and then showing you

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.050
some more complexity in
building it up over time.

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:49.180
So what we want with
configuration and management

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:51.170
of infrastructure
is a few things.

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:52.570
Kind of a very high level.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:55.030
One is you want
repeatable deployments.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:57.130
You want to know that
the code you're running

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:58.796
is going to match the
infrastructure you

00:05:58.796 --> 00:06:00.390
have in mind.

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:03.170
Next is you want to be
able to take that same code

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:06.800
and reproduce it in different
environments as you scale.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.400
And finally, but perhaps
most importantly,

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.330
you want to single
tool and model

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:14.310
to describe and
deploy these things.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.220
You don't want to have to change
what you do because you're now

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.420
deploying Google infrastructure
versus your own resources

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:22.300
or a third party's resources.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:26.240
And this is really where
Deployment Manager comes in.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:28.196
So what is Deployment Manager?

00:06:28.196 --> 00:06:31.000
Deployment Manager is our
tool in Google Cloud Platform

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:35.040
to create repeatable,
idempotent deployments.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:38.910
It's got a simple YAML-based--
so human readable-- description

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:42.110
language, with Jinja
and Python code behind.

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:44.800
So it's extremely flexible.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:48.080
And as I said before, it can
configure any GCP resource

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:48.580
type.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:50.600
So you're no longer
constrained to just VMs.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:54.700
We can do Kubernetes, AppEngine,
IP addresses, firewalls,

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.890
the whole range.

00:06:56.890 --> 00:07:02.450
And we've got a great glcoud
command line built in.

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:06.930
What Deployment Manager gives
you is resource configuration

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:08.730
that you can treat
like source code.

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:09.970
You can version control it.

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:11.120
You can share it.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:14.190
You can understand the
evolution over time.

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:17.972
And it uses these declarative
templates described in YAML.

00:07:17.972 --> 00:07:20.430
And what those give you are
these repeatable infrastructure

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:23.990
deployments that you use on
that gcloud command line.

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:27.130
All of these things
put together,

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:31.300
these templates and the
command line integration,

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:34.319
allows you to think at a much
higher level of abstraction.

00:07:34.319 --> 00:07:35.860
And in fact, as
we'll show you today,

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.220
you can start describing
parts of your architecture

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:41.520
as components, and start
using those and consuming

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:44.940
those components with higher
level building blocks.

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:46.780
All of this put
together gives you

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:49.662
what we call living
software deployments.

00:07:49.662 --> 00:07:51.370
And so I'm going to
turn it over to Ville

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.320
who's going to show us
Deployment Manager in action.

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:56.577
Can we switch over to the demo?

00:07:56.577 --> 00:07:57.660
VILLE AIKAS: Thanks, Anil.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:04.170
So I'm going to
go ahead and talk

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:07.835
a little bit about very basic
things from the beginning.

00:08:07.835 --> 00:08:09.460
We're all faced with
very simple things

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:12.140
like, how do I get my code
up and running in the cloud?

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:14.681
So there's a couple of ways
that you can do this today.

00:08:14.681 --> 00:08:17.180
As Anil mentioned, you can go
and go out there in the bakery

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:19.070
and you can say, hey,
give me an image.

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:21.320
Whether it's a docker image
or whether it's a VM image

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:22.236
doesn't really matter.

00:08:22.236 --> 00:08:24.510
For this example, I'm going
to be using VM images just

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:26.030
to keep things simple.

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:29.241
But once you have
that nicely big image,

00:08:29.241 --> 00:08:31.490
you want to go and get it
up and running in the cloud.

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:34.230
And we certainly have solutions
for you to do this today.

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:36.530
You can go up there
on the cloud console

00:08:36.530 --> 00:08:38.429
and you can go ahead
and turn some knobs

00:08:38.429 --> 00:08:42.360
and slide some sliders
and out pops a VM for you.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:43.380
And that's all great.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.440
But then if you want
to go and redo that,

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.770
say a week, two, five
weeks later, you come back

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:50.620
and you're like, what
was it that I do?

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:52.230
I can't remember.

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:56.340
You can also do this from
the gcloud compute tool.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:57.850
You can go in
there and say, hey,

00:08:57.850 --> 00:08:59.660
I would like to go in
and spin me up a VM,

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:02.740
give it some command line
flags, and out pops a VM.

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.722
But you're kind of faced
with the same problem again.

00:09:05.722 --> 00:09:07.180
The only thing that
you do have now

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:09.388
is maybe a command line
history, so you can hopefully

00:09:09.388 --> 00:09:10.390
spin back up to it.

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:13.279
But we can probably do a
little bit better on that.

00:09:13.279 --> 00:09:14.820
So I'm going to go
ahead and show you

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:18.750
how we can do this with the
Deployment Manager today.

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:21.110
So for the people
on Manager, you

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:23.290
go ahead and create
a configuration file.

00:09:23.290 --> 00:09:25.040
It's a YAML-based file.

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:26.210
It looks like this.

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:27.290
It's very simple.

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:31.420
At the top, you say, I would
like to create some resources.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:33.180
Well, that's nice.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:35.530
Then you say what you
would like to create

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:36.590
in the form of a list.

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:37.965
So in this case,
I want to create

00:09:37.965 --> 00:09:41.062
just a single VM called myvm.

00:09:41.062 --> 00:09:42.930
I give it a type,
because we need

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:44.900
to know what type
you're creating.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:47.140
This one is a VM, but
as Anil mentioned,

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:49.300
it could be just as well
a network, load balancer,

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:51.190
firewall, whatever it is.

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:53.750
And then we give it
the list of properties.

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:56.960
So the properties should
look familiar to you

00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:59.960
if you have used
compute in the past.

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:01.030
There's nothing new here.

00:10:01.030 --> 00:10:04.230
They all look and feel the
same because they are the same.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:06.360
Because the API is
mapped one to one

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:09.090
with being the underlying
resource and what

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.030
the Deployment
Manager talks about.

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:11.820
OK?

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:14.910
So I'll give it a
zone, a machine type.

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:18.640
This case, f1-micro
because it's a demo.

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:20.600
I give it a disk.

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:24.030
I want to boot off of
persistent disk using a source

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:26.270
image that I have baked
already for this demo.

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:28.932
It's creatively
called nodejsstatic,

00:10:28.932 --> 00:10:33.380
because I'm not very
good with naming.

00:10:33.380 --> 00:10:34.862
And then I have
network interface.

00:10:34.862 --> 00:10:36.320
I would like to
say, please give me

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:37.945
a network interface
that I can actually

00:10:37.945 --> 00:10:39.320
access from the internet.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:41.999
I say, give me a NATic
interface, one to one.

00:10:41.999 --> 00:10:44.540
That means that it's going to
give me an external IP address.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:50.420
So using a gcloud command
line tool, I can say,

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:55.270
Mr. DM Manager, please
give me a deployment.

00:10:57.830 --> 00:11:01.786
And I give it a pointer
to my YAML file.

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:07.386
Oh, I also have
to give it a name.

00:11:07.386 --> 00:11:10.700
So let's call it myvm.

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:11.340
OK.

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:13.870
So while that's going
on, the nice thing

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:16.080
is that because we
were able to go ahead,

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:18.310
once we created a YAML
file where we put all

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:20.210
these properties
into it, I can now

00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:22.709
go ahead and take
that file, and I

00:11:22.709 --> 00:11:24.000
can put it into source control.

