00:23:08.661 --> 00:23:10.660
I can create a MongoDB
because it doesn't really

00:23:10.660 --> 00:23:11.590
depend on anything.

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:13.750
But when it gets down
to the MongoDP endpoint,

00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:16.580
it's going to say,
oh, I need to go ahead

00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:21.630
and wait before creating this
service registry endpoint

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:23.600
because it depends on Mongo DB.

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:26.079
So DM is going to go
ahead and spin up the VM.

00:23:26.079 --> 00:23:27.620
And then it's going
to go and go back

00:23:27.620 --> 00:23:30.870
into that network
interface of that MongoDB

00:23:30.870 --> 00:23:32.450
and pull that out,
and then stuff it

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:33.550
in the service registry.

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:40.480
So because this
is a GCP resource,

00:23:40.480 --> 00:23:43.331
it actually means that it
behaves like a first class

00:23:43.331 --> 00:23:43.830
citizen.

00:23:43.830 --> 00:23:44.970
You can list your services.

00:23:44.970 --> 00:23:46.390
You can say, hey,
what are my services?

00:23:46.390 --> 00:23:48.830
I might have dev services,
I might have prod services,

00:23:48.830 --> 00:23:50.180
staging service, and so forth.

00:23:50.180 --> 00:23:52.970
I can create them and delete
them and everything else.

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:55.730
But the other nice thing that it
does behind the scenes for you

00:23:55.730 --> 00:24:04.530
is that it also creates a DNS
entry for you in your GCE DNS.

00:24:04.530 --> 00:24:07.030
So that means that without you
having to run your own naming

00:24:07.030 --> 00:24:08.860
and discovery
services, this endpoint

00:24:08.860 --> 00:24:10.950
will be visible
through you in the DNS,

00:24:10.950 --> 00:24:14.990
and you can just use this
from your VMs and Kubernetes

00:24:14.990 --> 00:24:17.300
clusters, and all of everything.

00:24:17.300 --> 00:24:21.690
And you can also restrict the
visibility of this endpoint

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:24.470
so that it's maybe only
visible to your dev network,

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:28.550
or test network, or whatnot.

00:24:28.550 --> 00:24:31.342
So let's go ahead
and spin that up.

00:24:44.750 --> 00:24:45.380
OK.

00:24:45.380 --> 00:24:48.260
So while that's going on
and it's creating that,

00:24:48.260 --> 00:24:51.490
so let's go and just step back
again and think about the value

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:52.760
that we are able to do now.

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:55.770
Because we are able to now be
able to decouple the production

00:24:55.770 --> 00:24:58.220
and consumption of these
services by using the service

00:24:58.220 --> 00:24:58.940
registry.

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:01.250
That's a really powerful
concept in itself.

00:25:01.250 --> 00:25:03.350
The second powerful
concept that we have here

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:05.600
is by building
these abstractions,

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:08.600
I can go ahead and very easily
and with a very high degree

00:25:08.600 --> 00:25:10.840
of confidence be able
to come in and say,

00:25:10.840 --> 00:25:12.560
when I need to bring
up my DevStack,

00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:15.185
no matter how complex it is--
because I have treated it

00:25:15.185 --> 00:25:16.640
as source code, right.

00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:17.850
It's checked in somewhere.

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:19.940
It's got tests
associated with it.

00:25:19.940 --> 00:25:22.120
Every time I just go and
spin up these stacks,

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:24.840
I know that they're
going to come up.

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:27.480
It also allows me to go and
nicely document these things.

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:29.330
With the JSON schema,
we saw those things.

00:25:29.330 --> 00:25:29.830
Right?

00:25:29.830 --> 00:25:32.540
So it was very clear and easy
to go and see how it's used

00:25:32.540 --> 00:25:33.460
and so forth.

00:25:33.460 --> 00:25:35.440
So there it is.

00:25:35.440 --> 00:25:37.830
It now comes back
and says, thank you.

00:25:37.830 --> 00:25:40.200
I created you two resources
like you asked me to.

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:43.430
So it created a MongoDB and it
also created a MongoDB endpoint

00:25:43.430 --> 00:25:44.056
for me.

00:25:46.790 --> 00:25:49.350
So let's go and see
what this looks like.

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:52.830
So in this case, I'm just going
to go and SSH into the machine

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:55.370
that I created in the first
case, my little single VM.

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:09.320
And I'll just grab this
name endpoint here.

00:26:09.320 --> 00:26:12.020
So if you look at this
name that we created here,

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:15.620
the nice thing about this
name is that it is carved out

00:26:15.620 --> 00:26:19.200
into its own namespace so
that I can easily go and say,

00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:20.660
what's the MongoDB enpoint?

00:26:20.660 --> 00:26:22.230
It's scoped to a project.

00:26:22.230 --> 00:26:26.300
And it's also separate
from your other DNS entries

00:26:26.300 --> 00:26:27.890
so that there's no
kind of collisions

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:30.835
between what other
people develop or add

00:26:30.835 --> 00:26:32.600
in there or anything else.

00:26:32.600 --> 00:26:34.460
So let me just grab
this from here.

00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:36.315
And I'm running in
the VM that I created

00:26:36.315 --> 00:26:39.500
that had no knowledge of
my handy dandy MongoDB

00:26:39.500 --> 00:26:40.330
even existing.

00:26:40.330 --> 00:26:46.016
I can now come in and I can
see it from my single VM,

00:26:46.016 --> 00:26:49.227
creating a nice decoupling
within my services.

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:54.128
Can we go back to the slides?

00:26:57.210 --> 00:26:58.310
OK.

00:26:58.310 --> 00:27:01.206
So let's go all the
way back and think

00:27:01.206 --> 00:27:02.330
about what we just started.

00:27:02.330 --> 00:27:05.910
So we started with a very
simple declarative YAML format.

00:27:05.910 --> 00:27:07.130
It was really nice and easy.

00:27:07.130 --> 00:27:08.730
We could just go
ahead and come in

00:27:08.730 --> 00:27:11.340
and look at all the properties
that we already know.

00:27:11.340 --> 00:27:14.680
If I know VMs, I can go and
come in and create my VMs

00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:16.490
using concepts I already know.

00:27:16.490 --> 00:27:17.970
OK.

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:19.470
And the second thing
we added on top

00:27:19.470 --> 00:27:22.320
of that was, OK, that's great,
but how do I parameterize that?

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:23.900
How do I create my own types?

00:27:23.900 --> 00:27:26.180
How do I add
programming in there?

00:27:26.180 --> 00:27:28.510
So we saw a little bit of
that using Jinja templates.

00:27:28.510 --> 00:27:30.380
Again, we also support Python.

00:27:30.380 --> 00:27:34.260
But these examples
are using Jinja.

00:27:34.260 --> 00:27:36.219
And we created new types.

00:27:36.219 --> 00:27:37.760
So this is a really
powerful concept,

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:39.801
because now what I can do
is, instead of thinking

00:27:39.801 --> 00:27:43.580
about my cloud resources
as just resources,

00:27:43.580 --> 00:27:46.500
I can start composing the
resources that I want to

00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:50.200
and put them into GitHub,
and describe and declare

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:53.270
what it is that they actually
do, how do they look like.

00:27:53.270 --> 00:27:54.450
I can test them.

00:27:54.450 --> 00:27:58.520
And I can really start creating
all of my configuration

00:27:58.520 --> 00:27:59.250
as code.

00:27:59.250 --> 00:28:01.520
It should be treated
just like code.

00:28:01.520 --> 00:28:03.090
You'll be happier.

00:28:03.090 --> 00:28:04.900
And then I also
showed you guys how

00:28:04.900 --> 00:28:07.790
we can go ahead and start
putting micro services,

00:28:07.790 --> 00:28:10.120
build on top of
those abstractions

00:28:10.120 --> 00:28:13.600
by using the service
registry as a loose coupling

00:28:13.600 --> 00:28:16.010
mechanism between the
producers of the service

00:28:16.010 --> 00:28:17.440
and the consumers
of the service.

00:28:21.100 --> 00:28:23.880
And so at this point, you
might say, OK, that was great.

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:26.000
You showed me a couple of
VMs and everything else,

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:28.660
but in reality, things are
really, really complex.

00:28:28.660 --> 00:28:29.160
Right?

00:28:29.160 --> 00:28:30.035
And I agree with you.

00:28:30.035 --> 00:28:32.250
They definitely
are very complex.

00:28:32.250 --> 00:28:37.310
So if you think about this
is a pretty standard kind

00:28:37.310 --> 00:28:40.005
of a pattern that we see
all the time, which is,

00:28:40.005 --> 00:28:43.010
I might have highly
available, autoscaled load

00:28:43.010 --> 00:28:46.960
balance thingamajigger
running, right?

00:28:46.960 --> 00:28:49.480
Let's call it my front end.

00:28:49.480 --> 00:28:52.370
How would I go and do something
like this in the cloud?

00:28:52.370 --> 00:28:53.970
It's pretty complex.

00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:57.530
Well, the nice thing is
that by us showing you

00:28:57.530 --> 00:29:01.020
how you can go ahead and create
and reuse some of these types,

00:29:01.020 --> 00:29:05.310
we've also created a bunch of
these best patterns and best

00:29:05.310 --> 00:29:07.380
practices that you
should be able to use

00:29:07.380 --> 00:29:08.340
right out of the gate.

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:10.640
And we have many of these
examples in our GitHub

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:14.010
that we'll show you
in just a second.

00:29:14.010 --> 00:29:17.660
And the second thing is that,
again, in the production,

00:29:17.660 --> 00:29:19.740
you probably don't
want to go ahead

00:29:19.740 --> 00:29:23.790
and-- even though you can
reliably go ahead and deploy

00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:26.049
your production architecture
up and down at will,

00:29:26.049 --> 00:29:27.840
you probably don't want
to go and tear down

00:29:27.840 --> 00:29:29.760
your entire infrastructure.

00:29:29.760 --> 00:29:32.980
Because your customers might
not be happy doing that.

00:29:32.980 --> 00:29:34.770
So we have a couple
of things that

00:29:34.770 --> 00:29:38.852
allow us to go ahead and update
parts of our infrastructure,

00:29:38.852 --> 00:29:39.810
like a rolling updater.

00:29:43.720 --> 00:29:46.628
And can I get back to my machine
just for a moment, please?

00:29:50.890 --> 00:29:51.810
Oh, that's big.

00:29:55.640 --> 00:29:56.770
OK.

00:29:56.770 --> 00:30:01.480
So what I have done here
