aspects that you care about,

00:35:39.580 --> 00:35:44.090
like for example, what version
of Mongo is it running?

00:35:44.090 --> 00:35:46.330
And how much is it
going to cost me

00:35:46.330 --> 00:35:48.164
in the specified configuration?

00:35:48.164 --> 00:35:49.580
And we actually
expose the pricing

00:35:49.580 --> 00:35:50.705
in a very detailed fashion.

00:35:50.705 --> 00:35:52.600
So you actually know
what you're paying

00:35:52.600 --> 00:35:54.680
for at the resource level.

00:35:54.680 --> 00:35:57.640
And we even show you your
sustained usage discount,

00:35:57.640 --> 00:35:59.840
which I know you all love
running on Google Cloud

00:35:59.840 --> 00:36:01.320
Platform.

00:36:01.320 --> 00:36:02.657
So that's great.

00:36:02.657 --> 00:36:04.240
Now we'll take a
look at what it looks

00:36:04.240 --> 00:36:08.530
like to deploy MongoDB
solution on Google Cloud

00:36:08.530 --> 00:36:10.240
Platform in the Launcher.

00:36:10.240 --> 00:36:11.940
So I just click Launch.

00:36:11.940 --> 00:36:14.330
And what you see is
not the same interface

00:36:14.330 --> 00:36:16.900
that you would get when
you're trying to deploy a VM.

00:36:16.900 --> 00:36:19.260
What you get is, instead,
something very tailored.

00:36:19.260 --> 00:36:22.720
Tailored specifically
to deploy Mongo.

00:36:22.720 --> 00:36:26.420
How many of you have
deployed Mongo yourselves?

00:36:26.420 --> 00:36:28.730
How many of you
enjoyed that process?

00:36:28.730 --> 00:36:29.230
Nobody.

00:36:29.230 --> 00:36:29.979
Not a single hand.

00:36:29.979 --> 00:36:31.244
Exactly.

00:36:31.244 --> 00:36:32.660
Totally relate to
your experience.

00:36:32.660 --> 00:36:35.210
This is really where I think
the power of the Launcher

00:36:35.210 --> 00:36:36.550
really shines.

00:36:36.550 --> 00:36:38.300
What we have here and
we're going to show

00:36:38.300 --> 00:36:41.720
you is how you can run
production configurations just

00:36:41.720 --> 00:36:43.830
by a few clicks in a wizard.

00:36:43.830 --> 00:36:45.520
So what we've done
here is we've exposed

00:36:45.520 --> 00:36:46.894
some of the key
things you'd want

00:36:46.894 --> 00:36:51.182
to configure for Mongo, namely
server nodes and arbiter nodes.

00:36:51.182 --> 00:36:53.140
So what you can do is
you can choose your zone.

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:54.540
That's great.

00:36:54.540 --> 00:36:56.950
And then you can choose
how may nodes you need.

00:36:56.950 --> 00:36:59.070
And so let's say I
want stick with two.

00:36:59.070 --> 00:37:01.440
And what we do is we have
intelligent defaults.

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:04.740
We sort of, over
our experiences,

00:37:04.740 --> 00:37:09.090
know what makes sense to run
different types of software.

00:37:09.090 --> 00:37:13.380
So in this case, we're
suggesting using a high mem 4

00:37:13.380 --> 00:37:14.684
vCPU machine.

00:37:14.684 --> 00:37:16.850
You can change that if you
don't like it, of course.

00:37:16.850 --> 00:37:18.308
But we're going to
stick with that.

00:37:18.308 --> 00:37:20.100
And since we're running
database software,

00:37:20.100 --> 00:37:21.516
you may want the
extra performance

00:37:21.516 --> 00:37:23.460
you get from running an SSD.

00:37:23.460 --> 00:37:24.960
And last but not
least, you probably

00:37:24.960 --> 00:37:27.690
want to control how much
storage you have attached.

00:37:27.690 --> 00:37:29.780
So we allow you to
control that as well.

00:37:29.780 --> 00:37:31.520
Well, in any MongoDB
configuration,

00:37:31.520 --> 00:37:33.445
as you can see here,
you have your primary

00:37:33.445 --> 00:37:34.900
and you have secondaries.

00:37:34.900 --> 00:37:36.885
And then you also
have this arbiter.

00:37:36.885 --> 00:37:40.089
Well, you want your primaries
and secondaries to be beefy,

00:37:40.089 --> 00:37:42.130
but that arbiter's not
doing a whole lot of work.

00:37:42.130 --> 00:37:44.590
All it's doing is kind of
being available for heartbeats

00:37:44.590 --> 00:37:46.460
and doing some leader election.

00:37:46.460 --> 00:37:48.877
And we've captured that
concept in our configuration.

00:37:48.877 --> 00:37:50.085
So what does it mean for you?

00:37:50.085 --> 00:37:51.751
That means that you're
not unnecessarily

00:37:51.751 --> 00:37:54.224
paying extra just because
you need another machine.

00:37:54.224 --> 00:37:56.890
We've instead suggested using an
f1-micro, the smallest instance

00:37:56.890 --> 00:38:00.230
size, to run that arbiter.

00:38:00.230 --> 00:38:01.970
I'll go ahead and click Deploy.

00:38:01.970 --> 00:38:03.670
And what you're
going to get taken to

00:38:03.670 --> 00:38:06.290
is a same experience
that Ville just showed,

00:38:06.290 --> 00:38:08.310
namely Deployment Manager.

00:38:08.310 --> 00:38:11.340
And so this week, we announced
that all of the packaged

00:38:11.340 --> 00:38:14.050
software solutions
available in Cloud Launcher

00:38:14.050 --> 00:38:16.949
are all built on
Deployment Manager.

00:38:16.949 --> 00:38:18.490
And what that means
is you get access

00:38:18.490 --> 00:38:20.840
to all the rich
resources and templates

00:38:20.840 --> 00:38:24.480
that Ville just described,
along with all of the power.

00:38:24.480 --> 00:38:26.510
So you can deploy once.

00:38:26.510 --> 00:38:28.670
Bring it down, tweak some items.

00:38:28.670 --> 00:38:30.510
And now get
repeatable deployments

00:38:30.510 --> 00:38:32.730
that you can integrate with
the rest of your software

00:38:32.730 --> 00:38:34.520
solutions.

00:38:34.520 --> 00:38:36.830
So this might take a
little bit to deploy,

00:38:36.830 --> 00:38:39.864
but I deployed one earlier
while I was watching

00:38:39.864 --> 00:38:41.030
another one of the sessions.

00:38:41.030 --> 00:38:42.796
I called it here gcp-next-mongo.

00:38:42.796 --> 00:38:44.420
And the things I want
to highlight here

00:38:44.420 --> 00:38:46.420
are, we're not just
giving you a deployment.

00:38:46.420 --> 00:38:48.295
We're not just bringing
up an infrastructure.

00:38:48.295 --> 00:38:51.200
What we're actually giving you
is a single pane of management

00:38:51.200 --> 00:38:52.960
for that infrastructure.

00:38:52.960 --> 00:38:54.396
So what do I mean by that?

00:38:54.396 --> 00:38:56.955
Well, if you're running MongoDB,
you have these primaries

00:38:56.955 --> 00:38:58.277
and you have this arbiter.

00:38:58.277 --> 00:38:59.860
One of the first
things you want to do

00:38:59.860 --> 00:39:03.050
after you deploy this
software is start using it.

00:39:03.050 --> 00:39:05.140
And so what we've done
is we've bubbled up

00:39:05.140 --> 00:39:09.120
the primary actions
directly on this page.

00:39:09.120 --> 00:39:11.400
So for this MongoDB
solution, I see

00:39:11.400 --> 00:39:14.130
that I can have a button
that's SSH-ing to the primary

00:39:14.130 --> 00:39:15.770
and the server.

00:39:15.770 --> 00:39:19.640
And this is taking
advantage of our cloud SSH

00:39:19.640 --> 00:39:22.507
feature, which allow you to
not have to worry about keys.

00:39:22.507 --> 00:39:24.090
Everyone-- there's
not a single person

00:39:24.090 --> 00:39:25.340
who doesn't love this feature.

00:39:25.340 --> 00:39:26.906
I know all of you relate to it.

00:39:26.906 --> 00:39:27.530
So here we are.

00:39:27.530 --> 00:39:29.720
Now we're in our machine
and this is the primary.

00:39:29.720 --> 00:39:31.340
So out of those three
instances, we've

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:33.540
already shown you
how to get exactly

00:39:33.540 --> 00:39:35.995
to where you want to be to
begin with, the primary.

00:39:35.995 --> 00:39:38.110
And I'm going to show
you it running in action.

00:39:38.110 --> 00:39:41.240
So I'm just going to
go Mongo local host.

00:39:41.240 --> 00:39:43.350
And since this is a
replica set, there

00:39:43.350 --> 00:39:45.490
